The Vouvray and Montlouis vineyards are two appellations, one grape and the Loire between them. From our terrace in Nazelles-Négron, we are closer to the cellars than to the centre of Amboise: Noizay, one of the villages of the Vouvray appellation, is right next door. I am Italian, I grew up with other wines, and it is here that I learnt to love Chenin Blanc. Here is how I advise our guests to visit these two vineyards.

## Vouvray and Montlouis: two vineyards face to face

Vouvray and Montlouis-sur-Loire are two appellations d'origine contrôlée (AOC) of Touraine, set on either bank of the Loire between Tours and Amboise. They grow a single white grape, Chenin Blanc, and produce dry, off-dry and sweet wines as well as sparkling ones.

Vouvray is on the north bank. The appellation, recognised in 1936 according to the INAO, covers several villages of the hillside, from Rochecorbon to Noizay by way of Vouvray, Vernou-sur-Brenne and Chançay. It is the larger and better known of the two.

Montlouis-sur-Loire faces it on the south bank. Recognised in 1938, the appellation covers three villages: Montlouis-sur-Loire, Lussault-sur-Loire and Saint-Martin-le-Beau. Smaller, it counts many independent winemakers, and you often deal with the person who made the wine.

For a stay, the difference is mostly practical: you can do Vouvray in the morning, cross the Loire at Amboise and finish at Montlouis in the afternoon.

![Poplars of the Loire valley at the end of the season, bare branches and mistletoe against an orange sky](/images/inline/chateau-exterieur/chateau-exterieur-09.webp "The poplars of the valley, at the edge of the grounds. The slopes of Vouvray begin a few kilometres further on.")

## What to drink: dry, sweet or sparkling?

The Chenin Blanc of Vouvray and Montlouis comes in four styles: dry, off-dry, sweet and sparkling. The label always says which, and it is the first thing to check before buying.

### Dry, for the table

Dry Vouvray and dry Montlouis are lively, with notes of apple, quince and sometimes honey. They go with rillettes de Tours, a Loire fish or a creamy dish. It is what I serve most often with my cooking.

### Off-dry and sweet, for cheese and dessert

The off-dry keeps a touch of sugar that the acidity of the Chenin balances. With an aged Sainte-Maure-de-Touraine, it is the pairing I recommend to all our guests. Sweet and fully sweet wines come from hot years, when the grapes ripen late on the vine; they age for decades, and are opened with dessert or foie gras.

### Sparkling, for the aperitif

The bubbles of Vouvray and Montlouis are made like champagne, through a second fermentation in the bottle. The lightly sparkling "pétillant" has less pressure than the fully sparkling "mousseux", so a finer, softer bubble. In Touraine, it is the aperitif wine, and a very good alternative to champagne at a much gentler price.

## How to visit the wine cellars of Vouvray

The cellars of Vouvray are dug into the tufa, the white stone of the hillside, and stay at around twelve degrees all year. The big houses and the Cave des Producteurs de Vouvray offer guided visits with a tasting; independent winemakers mostly receive by appointment.

Here is what we tell our guests before they set off:

- **Call the day before** an independent winemaker. Many work alone and are not at the cellar door at all hours, especially during the harvest.
- **Three cellars a day, no more.** Beyond that the wines blur together and the day becomes a race.
- **Bring a light jumper**, even in August: the cellar stays cool when it is 30 °C outside.
- **Choose a designated driver**, or spit. The hillside roads are narrow and the gendarmerie knows them well.
- **The tasting is often free** at winemakers' when you buy, and paid for guided visits at the big cellars. Ask about the price when you book.

If rain sets in, a cellar is the best plan there is. Our guide to [things to do in Amboise](/en/blog/things-to-do-amboise) has more ideas for a grey day.

## Montlouis-sur-Loire, the winemakers' vineyard

Montlouis-sur-Loire is mostly visited at the winemakers' own cellars, which are smaller than those of Vouvray, and often in the company of the person who makes the wine. The vineyard sits on the plateau and the hillside above the Loire, about twenty minutes from the château.

La Loire à Vélo runs through Montlouis, and it is our favourite way of discovering it: a loop of about thirty kilometres from Amboise, with a tasting in the middle. The route is described in our article on [cycling the Loire from Amboise](/en/blog/loire-valley-cycling-amboise). We do not hire out bikes, but some bike hire companies in Amboise deliver to the hotel.

In September and October, part of the vineyard is harvested by hand, and the hillside smells of must. It is the loveliest season to come, provided you accept that the winemakers have less time for you.

## What to eat with a Vouvray?

With a Vouvray, eat the Touraine way: rillettes and rillons with a dry, aged Sainte-Maure with an off-dry, tarte aux pommes or poire tapée with a sweet. Andouillette au Vouvray can still be found in the bistros of the region.

I describe these pairings in more detail in our article on [Touraine food specialities](/en/blog/touraine-food-specialities). My cooking remains Italian, but I think it through with the wines from here, and Chenin Blanc goes very well with a risotto or a burrata.

![Guests seated at the table in the château, candles lit and a bunch of flowers on the cloth](/images/inline/restauration/restauration-06.webp "A dinner at the château's table. Our guests often open the bottle they brought back from the cellar.")

## Bringing your bottles back to the château

You can open at the château the bottles you bought during the day. When we provide the meal, corkage is €8 per bottle. The château's table serves resident guests from 1 May to 30 September, and on Thursdays the dinner is Italian; the rest of the year, it opens on request for six people or more. The details are on our [dining](/en/chateau-dining-touraine) page.

In winter, a sweet Vouvray is lovely by the fireplace in the lounge, after a day among the cellars.

![Lit stone fireplace in a lounge of the château, candles on the mantelpiece](/images/inline/salons-interieurs/salons-interieurs-03.webp "The lounge fireplace, in winter. The right place to taste a sweet wine.")

## Where to sleep between Vouvray and Amboise

To visit the Vouvray and Montlouis vineyards, sleep on the north bank, between Amboise and Vouvray. The Château de la Huberdière, a charming 3-star hotel with 10 bedrooms, is in Nazelles-Négron, next to Noizay, a quarter of an hour from the Vouvray hillside and 2 hrs 20 from Paris.

The walled park covers 14 hectares, and the lap pool is heated from April to October to finish the afternoon. The homemade breakfast is €15 per person. To compare the neighbourhoods and the options around town, read our guide on [where to stay in Amboise](/en/blog/where-to-stay-amboise).

For a weekend among the cellars of Touraine, discover our rooms and [book your stay at the château](/en/boutique-hotel-amboise): we answer every request within 24 hours.
